his forces White's king to move (likely to a3, b2, or b1), which disrupts White's position and gives me the initiative. After the king moves, my knight will be actively placed and can continue to harass White's position. This is more forcing than N: a1-c2, which while attacking the bishop, allows White more flexibility in response. In chess, forcing moves that put pressure on the opponent's king are generally preferred when available.\n\n**Answer:** N: a1-b3"}
