g to challenge it.\n\nReason: **B: b7-d5** is the strongest move because:\n- It centralizes my bishop on an excellent square\n- Controls both central and kingside squares (e4, f3, g2, c6, e6)\n- Restricts White's knight from occupying key squares\n- Maintains flexibility - my king can later move to d6 to deal with the b6 pawn\n- The bishop on d5 supports potential pawn advances (f6-f5 or g7-g5/g6)\n- From d5, the bishop can quickly reposition if needed\n\nAnswer: B: b7-d5"}
